{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Strategy: observe that both sides of each `Iff` are independently true, so prove the four universal assertions separately and then assemble the conjunction.

- Right-adjoint uniqueness: fix \(b\). Each adjunction identifies \(a \le g_i(b)\) with \(f(a)\le b\). Substituting \(a=g_1(b)\) and \(a=g_2(b)\) gives both inequalities between \(g_1(b)\) and \(g_2(b)\); antisymmetry in \(\alpha\) gives pointwise equality, hence \(g_1=g_2\).

- Countable-sup closure: represent any nonempty countable subset by a sequence \(\mathbb N\to\alpha\), repeating a chosen element if necessary. Its supremum is the subset’s LUB, so the sequence hypothesis yields `CountableSupClosed`.

- LUB invariance: show
  \[
  \operatorname{upperBounds}(\operatorname{countableSupClosure}(s))
  =\operatorname{upperBounds}(s).
  \]
  Indeed, every new element is a LUB of a nonempty countable subset of \(s\), hence is bounded by every upper bound of \(s\). Equality of upper-bound sets immediately gives the stated `IsLUB` equivalence.

- Sup-closure after countable-inf closure: represent two closure elements as \(\inf_i a_i\) and \(\inf_j b_j\), with all \(a_i,b_j\in s\). In a coframe,
  \[
  (\inf_i a_i)\vee(\inf_j b_j)=\inf_{i,j}(a_i\vee b_j).
  \]
  The terms \(a_i\vee b_j\) lie in \(s\) by `SupClosed`, and the product index is again nonempty countable. Thus the result lies in `countableInfClosure s`.

These mechanisms correspond directly to the library results `CountableSupClosed.of_iSup_mem`, `isLUB_countableSupClosure`, and `SupClosed.countableInfClosure`. The partial-order assumption supplies the essential antisymmetry in the first argument, while the coframe law supplies the essential distributivity in the last. [Mathlib documentation](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/CountableSupClosed.html)"}}
